# One character in the search box does not draw the whole catalog.
#
# The sidebar used to render every match of a live query uncapped, on the
# reasoning that a filter has already narrowed the list. That is true of a
# query that narrows to a handful and false of the FIRST KEYSTROKE, which
# narrows nothing — and the first keystroke is the moment the list is
# guaranteed to be at its longest. At a 2020-session catalog, typing one letter
# built 10,615 entities and cost 18 ms a frame; at 20,000 it was 213 ms, which
# is four frames a second and reads as a freeze.
#
# This is the only scripted test that runs against a BIG catalog.
# HANABI_STRESS_SESSIONS appends synthetic rows to the mock, which every other
# script deliberately avoids — a fixture that changed size would move every
# coordinate-pinned assertion and every parity score. Here the size IS the
# subject: 320 rows, nearly all of which contain "e", and only a viewport's
# worth of them may be drawn.
#
# HANABI_ROW_AUDIT prints "sidebar rows <drawn> of <matched>", because the gap
# between those two numbers is the entire claim and nothing else exposes it. A
# script can read a label and not a row it cannot see, and the "Show N more"
# row that would say the list was capped rides at the bottom of TWO viewports
# by construction (fillCap = viewportRows * 2), so it is never on screen
# without scrolling.
#
# The 38 is a function of the window height, which is why this script pins one.
# If it changes, re-measure rather than nudge: run the app at 1180x949 with
# HANABI_ROW_AUDIT=1 and read the label.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1180,"window_height":949,"open_tabs":[],"active_tab":"","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_WIN_W=1180 HANABI_WIN_H=949 HANABI_STRESS_SESSIONS=300 HANABI_ROW_AUDIT=1

# The control: with no query the flat list is already capped, and 320 rows
# come down to a viewport's worth.
expect_text "sidebar rows 29 of 320 @ 0"

# Type one character. "e" is in almost every synthetic title (the verbs are
# "profiling", "migrating", "sweeping", "reindexing", "draining",
# "backfilling", "reconciling", "compacting"), so this is the widest useful
# query — the shape of a first keystroke.
click 140 283
wait_frames 4
type "e"
wait_frames 10

# The rows that matched are real rows.
expect_text "reindexing"
# Not the no-results state: this matches most of the catalog.
expect_no_text "No matches"

# And here is the fix: still 38 drawn, now against 299 matched. Before it, a
# live query set the limit to the total and all 299 were built -- the label
# would have read "sidebar rows 299 of 299".
expect_text "sidebar rows 38 of 299 @ 0"
